[Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Wednesday November 9th I'm Zoe Thomas  for The Wall Street Journal  imagine you got a text message out of  the blue from an unknown number  something like sorry I'm running late or  are we going to the salon tonight  maybe you tell that person they've got  the wrong number  but any kind of response could open you  up to a scam that's cost people Millions  so how can a seemingly innocuous text go  on to become a massive possibly  life-changing Shakedown  joining us to discuss this is our cyber  security reporter Robert McMillan hi Bob  thanks for coming back on Tech news  briefing hi Zoe very happy to be here  okay so we know this starts with an out  of the blue message from a stranger  well you get it's like a wrong number  right I have a dog that needs to be seen  by a vet can you help me right away and  then you write back and you say I'm  sorry I think you have the wrong number  and then very quickly you get like a  photograph of a young attractive looking  person and a couple of messages just  saying oh silly me you know I have the  sick animal and sort of a couple of  things that are like lures to see if  you'll keep the conversation going now  if you don't respond then it's over but  if they've somehow intrigued you it  becomes the beginning of what seems to  be sort of an innocuous conversation  with a stranger over the Internet okay  so once you have a relationship with the  stranger that's developed over text or I  assume over other messaging services as  well yeah it's not just text although  those are the ones I seem to be getting  the most uh but you could get one of  these messages via Facebook LinkedIn is  actually a pretty popular way to do it  because they can sort of mine your  LinkedIn information and then 10 that  they worked at the same company as you  or went to the same College as you so  you get these messages and the  relationship might build over a couple  of days or up to a week and then they  pull the trigger and try to get you to  buy crypto crypto huh I mean it feels  like these days everybody's trying to  get me to buy crypto Bob so how do these  scammers make that transition how do  they convince these strangers to buy  crypto all right to be perfectly clear  I'm not trying to get you to buy crypto  so what happens is just in the course of  talking about who you are and who they  are they will present themselves as very  financially successful and secure people  who have made millions of dollars using  these trading systems and they'll start  asking you have you ever heard of  cryptocurrency have you ever wondered  about it and ideally you're somebody  who's maybe heard of it but doesn't know  that much about it and so then they  become kind of your crypto Sherpa  encouraging you and walking you through  the process of first of all acquiring it  and then ultimately sending it to them  as part of a scam you buy some crypto  probably on a legitimate exchange but  then they get you to transfer it to a  fake exchange a website that they  control where it looks like you have a  brokerage account and it looks like  you're making tons of money on this fake  website ultimately to get your money  back they start imposing a bunch of  things that cost you more money so  there's a fee that you have to pay or  you have to pay taxes and so the idea is  the more that you trust this person the  more you are invested in just paying  that extra hundred thousand dollars that  little bit more to get your money out  and to make the whole thing end does  this kind of scam have a name oh this is  called Pig butchering because the  scammers fatten up their victims first  wow  you know you spoke to some of the  victims of these crimes who are they and  what did they say their experience was  like well they tend to be people with  money you know so middle-aged people  maybe a little bit you know empty  nesters maybe a little bit kind of  looking for somebody to chat with the  victims I spoke with were all  professionals you know they were smart  people but people who were neophytes in  the world of cryptocurrency and I think  a lot of them felt like there was this  trend that had passed them by now it's  interesting to note that a lot of  victims were hit when crypto was on the  way up and people were feeling like they  were missing out and what law  enforcement investigators tell me now is  now that there's sort of a crypto winter  and everybody's losing money on crypto  the pitch has become well you can short  sell the crypto so you can actually make  money on the decline of crypto so they  get you either way  Bob one of the victims Jane Yan who you  interviewed also spoke with our sister  podcast the journal this is how she  described feeling after losing 1.6  million dollars in the scam  it's been six months now I'm doing a  little better than before  [Music]  my anxiety level is still high I Wake  Middle night and my heart aches  it just pain there even I try to say  nothing about it and try to say I'm  going to be moving forward  but the thoughts still there you know I  feel it's it's very painful  we saved for our life just hope we have  a secure future  you know not handing over to a criminal  you know she sounds obviously devastated  but it also sounds like these scammers  are you know quite intelligent the idea  that they're sort of shifting their  pitch what do we know about them  well there are a couple of groups that  have investigated them and what they  have told me is that they are largely  based out of Asia that some of the  scammers work in these sort of compounds  they're almost like free trade zones  where you can set up a business so often  Chinese run maybe working out of  Cambodia and they have just like little  office Towers you know filled with  scammer employees who are sending out  these pitches they're often following a  script that is very carefully crafted  they will do things that you might not  expect from a scammer like if you want  to talk to them on the phone they'll do  that if you want to have a video  conference they'll do that too could  this scam exist without cryptocurrency  oh yeah I mean crypto is like the latest  Twist on it but these kind of scams have  been around for a while they're called  confidence scams sometimes they're  called romance scams the FBI says that  the broad category of all these scams  took in nearly a billion dollars last  year from the U.S victims who reported  it if it wasn't crypto they would be  convincing you that they were in trouble  in London and they needed you to get a  bunch of Amazon gift cards sent to them  or wired the money crypto is just  extremely fast it's a great way of  moving money across International  borders and it's become very easy to use  so you don't actually have to even leave  your home to become swindled by this so  then how difficult is it for a law  enforcement to stop these kinds of scams  or to hold the scammers accountable oh  it's like so much of the criminal  activity on the internet like it often  occurs in jurisdictions where U.S law  enforcement really has a hard time or  can't even reach the perpetrators so  they're not going to be able to stop it  the thing that's interesting to me is  that law enforcement has had some  success in recovering funds crypto funds  because you can send the money very  quickly the law enforcement  investigators can trace it back very  quickly and if the money ends up in an  exchange we would just legitimately run  law enforcement can send in a node and  say hey freeze this money don't send it  anywhere else we think it's illegally  obtained and they've had some success  not a ton but some success actually  getting money back all right that's our  cyber security reporter Robert McMillan  Bob thanks so much for joining us always  a pleasure to talk to you  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing if you want more Tech stories  check out our website  wsj.com I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall  Street Journal thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]

